@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# Summary
+
+## Changed
+- `evlog/cli.py`: new `report --since DATE` option. It accepts `YYYY-MM-DD` or an ISO 8601 datetime. A value with no UTC offset is taken as UTC, so a bare date means midnight UTC. A bad value gives an argparse usage error (exit code 2), not a traceback.
+- `evlog/events.py`: added `as_utc()` and `filter_since()`. Events are compared as timezone-aware instants, so offset timestamps are converted correctly. Naive legacy timestamps count as UTC, as the README says. The comparison is inclusive (`>=`).
+- `README.md`: documented `--since`.
+- `tests/test_since.py` (new): covers the UTC-midnight boundary with mixed offsets and naive timestamps, an offset datetime value, and a bad value giving exit code 2 with a clear message. No existing tests were touched.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest`: 5 tests, all OK. This includes the teammate's `test_since_excludes_older_days`.
+- Manual runs with a one-event file dated 2024-03-12:
+  - `--since 2024-13-45` printed `evlog report: error: argument --since: invalid date '2024-13-45'; ...` and no traceback.
+  - `--since 2024-03-10` printed `a x 1` and `total 1`.
+  - `--since 2024-03-13` printed `total 0`.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+- I assumed a date-only `--since` means midnight UTC. Ops might expect their local midnight. This is easy to change if so.
+- A corrupt events file (bad JSON, missing fields) still raises a traceback. That is existing behaviour and outside this request.
+- I only ran it on the Python version installed here. `datetime.fromisoformat` accepts fewer formats on older Pythons (before 3.11).
